kosha chirality

  -- a molecule is chiral if it cannot be superimposed on its mirror image.
  -- the most common source: a carbon bonded to 4 different groups (chiral centre).
  -- the two non-superimposable mirror images are enantiomers.
  -- identical physical properties except: rotate polarised light in opposite directions.
  -- R/S nomenclature (Cahn-Ingold-Prelog): priority rules assign R or S to each centre.
  --
  -- biological significance is profound:
  --   all natural amino acids are L (left-handed) — not D.
  --   all natural sugars are D (right-handed) — not L.
  --   enzymes are chiral — they only bind one enantiomer.
  --   most drugs are chiral — only one enantiomer is active.
  --   thalidomide: one enantiomer sedative, the other teratogenic.
  --
  -- diastereomers: stereoisomers that are NOT mirror images.
  --   n chiral centres → up to 2^n stereoisomers.
  -- racemic mixture: equal amounts of R and S. optically inactive.
  --
  -- chirality is the kosha's polarity at the molecular level —
  -- same atoms, same bonds, but the left hand is not the right hand.

